---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Counter from "../components/Counter.jsx";
---

<Layout title="Learning | My Astro Journey">
  <section class="learning-header">
    <p class="eyebrow">MY LEARNING</p>

    <h1>What I've Learned with Astro</h1>

    <p class="intro">
      This page documents the Astro concepts I've learned while building
      this website step by step.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="learning-layout">
    <aside class="sidebar">
      <h2>Topics</h2>

      <nav>
        <a href="#basics">Astro Basics</a>
        <a href="#components">Components</a>
        <a href="#pages">Pages & Routing</a>
        <a href="#css">CSS</a>
        <a href="#assets">Assets</a>
        <a href="#collections">Content Collections</a>
        <a href="#dynamic-routes">Dynamic Routes</a>
        <a href="#rendering">Rendering Markdown</a>
        <a href="#tags">Tags</a>
        <a href="#rss">RSS</a>
      </nav>
    </aside>

    <main class="documentation">
      <section id="basics">
        <p class="topic-number">01</p>
        <h2>Astro Basics</h2>

        <p>
          Astro is a web framework designed for building fast and modern
          websites. An Astro project contains pages, components, layouts,
          styles, assets, and other files.
        </p>

        <p>
          One important thing I learned is that Astro can generate most of
          the HTML on the server instead of sending unnecessary JavaScript
          to the browser.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="components">
        <p class="topic-number">02</p>
        <h2>Components</h2>

        <p>
          Components are reusable pieces of a website. Instead of writing
          the same HTML again and again, I can create a component and use
          it wherever I need it.
        </p>

        <pre><code>&lt;LearningCard
  title="Astro Basics"
  description="Learning the fundamentals of Astro."
/&gt;</code></pre>

        <p>
          In this project, <code>LearningCard.astro</code> is a reusable
          component used on the home page.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="pages">
        <p class="topic-number">03</p>
        <h2>Pages & Routing</h2>

        <p>
          Astro uses file-based routing. Files inside the
          <code>src/pages</code> directory automatically become routes.
        </p>

        <pre><code>src/pages/
├── index.astro
├── about.astro
├── learning.astro
└── blog.astro</code></pre>

        <p>
          For example, <code>about.astro</code> becomes the
          <code>/about</code> page.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="css">
        <p class="topic-number">04</p>
        <h2>Global CSS & Component CSS</h2>

        <p>
          I learned that Astro supports both global CSS and
          component-scoped CSS.
        </p>

        <p>
          Global CSS is useful for styles that should apply across the
          entire website, such as typography, colors, navigation, and
          general layout.
        </p>

        <p>
          Component-scoped CSS is useful when a style belongs only to one
          component or page.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="assets">
        <p class="topic-number">05</p>
        <h2>Assets</h2>

        <p>
          Astro provides different ways to work with images and other
          static files.
        </p>

        <p>
          Files inside <code>public</code> can be referenced directly by
          their URL, while files inside <code>src/assets</code> can be
          processed and optimized by Astro.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="collections">
        <p class="topic-number">06</p>
        <h2>Content Collections</h2>

        <p>
          Content collections help organize structured content such as
          blog posts.
        </p>

        <p>
          In this project, my Markdown blog posts are stored inside:
        </p>

        <pre><code>src/content/blog/</code></pre>

        <p>
          I also created a content schema to define the expected fields
          such as title, description, tags, and publication date.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="dynamic-routes">
        <p class="topic-number">07</p>
        <h2>Dynamic Routes</h2>

        <p>
          Dynamic routes allow one page to generate multiple URLs based on
          data.
        </p>

        <p>
          For example, the file:
        </p>

        <pre><code>src/pages/blog/[slug].astro</code></pre>

        <p>
          can generate pages such as:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>/blog/learning-astro</li>
          <li>/blog/my-first-website</li>
          <li>/blog/understanding-components</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          I learned that <code>getStaticPaths()</code> is used to tell
          Astro which dynamic pages should be generated.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="rendering">
        <p class="topic-number">08</p>
        <h2>Rendering Markdown</h2>

        <p>
          Astro's content API provides a <code>render()</code> function
          that can convert a Markdown content entry into renderable
          content.
        </p>

        <pre><code>const &#123; Content &#125; = await render(post);</code></pre>

        <p>
          The resulting <code>Content</code> component can then be placed
          inside the page.
        </p>

        <pre><code>&lt;Content /&gt;</code></pre>
      </section>

      <section id="tags">
        <p class="topic-number">09</p>
        <h2>Tags</h2>

        <p>
          I used the tags stored in each blog post to create tag pages.
        </p>

        <p>
          For example, posts can have tags such as:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>astro</li>
          <li>web-development</li>
          <li>components</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          Astro can use these tags to generate pages showing all posts
          associated with a particular tag.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="rss">
        <p class="topic-number">10</p>
        <h2>RSS Feeds</h2>

        <p>
          I also learned how to create an RSS feed for my blog.
        </p>

        <p>
          My RSS feed is available at:
        </p>

        <pre><code>/rss.xml</code></pre>

        <p>
          The feed contains information about my blog posts, including
          their titles, descriptions, dates, and links.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
  <section class="island-demo">
  <h2>My First Astro Island</h2>

  <p>
    This counter is interactive because it runs as a client-side island.
  </p>

  <Counter client:load />
</section>
   <section class="island-demo">
  <h2>Day & Night Mode</h2>

  <p>
    This button is a React component running as an Astro island.
  </p>
</section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .learning-header {
    max-width: 750px;
    margin-bottom: 4rem;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    color: #64748b;
  }

  .learning-header h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem);
    line-height: 1.05;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
  }

  .intro {
    max-width: 650px;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }

  .learning-layout {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 220px 1fr;
    gap: 4rem;
    align-items: start;
  }

  .sidebar {
    position: sticky;
    top: 2rem;
  }

  .sidebar h2 {
    margin-top: 0;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  .sidebar nav {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .sidebar nav a {
    color: #64748b;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .sidebar nav a:hover {
    color: #0f172a;
  }

  .documentation {
    max-width: 750px;
  }

  .documentation section {
    padding-bottom: 4rem;
    margin-bottom: 4rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #e2e8f0;
  }

  .documentation section:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .topic-number {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: #94a3b8;
  }

  .documentation h2 {
    margin-top: 0;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    font-size: 2rem;
  }

  .documentation p {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .documentation ul {
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
    color: #64748b;
  }

  .documentation li {
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }

  code {
    padding: 0.15rem 0.35rem;
    border-radius: 0.25rem;
    background: #e2e8f0;
    color: #0f172a;
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }

  pre {
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    padding: 1.25rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    border-radius: 0.75rem;
    background: #0f172a;
  }

  pre code {
    padding: 0;
    background: transparent;
    color: #e2e8f0;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .learning-layout {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 2rem;
    }

    .sidebar {
      position: static;
    }

    .sidebar nav {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
      gap: 0.75rem;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 500px) {
    .sidebar nav {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .documentation h2 {
      font-size: 1.6rem;
    }
  }

  .island-demo {
  margin-top: 2rem;
  padding: 2rem;
  border: 1px solid #e2e8f0;
  border-radius: 0.75rem;
  background: white;
}

.island-demo h2 {
  margin-top: 0;
}

.island-demo button {
  padding: 0.7rem 1rem;
  border: 1px solid #cbd5e1;
  border-radius: 0.5rem;
  background: #0f172a;
  color: white;
  font-size: 0.95rem;
  cursor: pointer;
}

.island-demo button:hover {
  opacity: 0.9;
}
   
  body.dark-mode .island-demo {
  background: #1e293b;
  border-color: #334155;
}

body.dark-mode .island-demo button {
  background: #e2e8f0;
  color: #0f172a;
}

body.dark-mode .documentation section {
  border-color: #334155;
}

body.dark-mode .sidebar nav a {
  color: #94a3b8;
}

body.dark-mode .sidebar nav a:hover {
  color: white;
}
</style>